h no new tradeoff. One complete journey can suffice only when no other eligible choice supplies a useful comparator. For an incomplete-only answer, choose leads whose reported date, seats, fees or program show a useful next-check contrast; these remain leads, never complete itineraries. Preserve unknown price scope, traveler support and separate-ticket protection. The writer sees only the selected source facts, so retain material comparison evidence. The exact_details index supplies source-bound journey_components, per-journey separate-booking waits, factored award-itinerary connections, and component original-currency quotes in major units. Use these exact references to distinguish within-award layovers from separate-ticket connection waits; they have different risk meanings. Compare reported fee and cash amounts using the display value and currency, never mental division of raw minor units. A quote with unknown price_scope is neither per traveler nor a party total, even if returned traveler evidence is known. Unknown wait_minutes is not zero. Keep a comparator when its exact buffer, schedule, cabin or quote changes the decision; no fixed count applies. For each selected choice, also return purpose: one short editorial hypothesis about why this exact choice helps the traveler decide, and compare_to: another selected exact alias or null. The output choice is {k:'j'|'b'|'l',r:exact alias,purpose:short text,compare_to:exact selected other alias or null}. Choose a comparison target when it helps reveal a concrete supported gain and real loss; otherwise null. Describe an intended traveler decision in purpose, not a new source value, repeated number or invented traveler preference. Do not choose near-identical siblings merely to fill the pool. If a same-program, same-cabin cousin looks worse on schedule and price, select it only for a source-supported offsetting traveler benefit. These purposes are unverified writing suggestions, not source facts or final recommendations